  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 236 PM CDT WED JUL 29 2026\n\nImpressive dewpoints around the area today, with upper 70s and\nnear 80 degrees quite common. Fortunately, a persistent cloud \ndeck was helping to keep air temperatures in check and heat \nindices from getting out of control. Will maintain the current \nHeat Advisory through its 9 pm expiration.\n\nWith the upper level dome of high pressure centered to our west,\nwe will remain in a northwest flow regime through the rest of the\nwork week. Drier air (in a relative sense) is forecast to push \ninto the area from the north and northeast, which will limit the\nchances of rain to the southern part of the CWA. There's still a\nMarginal Risk of severe storms in the far southeast through this\nevening. But based on radar trends so far this afternoon, the more\nintense convection should stay to our south and southeast.\n\nModels are still advertising a shortwave trough to dig into the\nTennessee and lower Ohio Valleys late Friday and into the weekend.\nLift associated with this trough will likely cause enhanced rain\nchances Friday night into Saturday. Latest computer model runs\nseem to be a little faster bringing this feature through than\nbefore. So, Sunday may not be as much of a washout as previously\nthought.\n\nBeyond that, increasing model spread in how the upper level low is\nhandled leads to lower forecast confidence early next week.\nComputer model blend schemes output POPs and temperatures near\nclimo levels, and that quite honestly seems like the best course\nof action at this time.\n\n/61/",
